<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karabakh movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1988">1988</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> movement was a mass Armenian movement from 1988 to 1991 demanding the transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a> from Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> to Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Huge demonstrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>, led by the Karabakh Committee, turned it into a broader campaign for democracy and independence, and its leaders, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levon-ter-petrosyan">Levon Ter-Petrosyan</a>, came to power in Armenia in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karabakh-movement.jpg" alt="Karabakh movement" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ռուսլան Սարգսյան · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artsakh_Movement,_February_13,_1988.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a> was the only autonomous region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> whose ethnic majority matched that of a neighbouring republic but which was not allowed to join it. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> complained of economic neglect, the closing of churches and schools, limits on teaching Armenian history and the settling of Azerbaijanis in the region; their share of its population fell from 84.4% in 1959 to 76% twenty years later. In periods of change, as in 1945, 1965 and 1977, they petitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> for transfer to Soviet Armenia, and in August 1987 some 75,000 Armenians signed a petition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The movement is traditionally dated from a demonstration in Stepanakert on 13 February 1988. On 20 February the Soviet of the autonomous oblast voted 110 to 17 to ask for the region's transfer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, which the journalist Thomas de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waal">Waal</a> called truly revolutionary, and crowds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>'s Theater Square doubled day by day. In March activists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> founded Krunk, which used strikes as a political weapon, and the Karabakh Committee of intellectuals was formed in Yerevan in May.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> refused to change the borders: the Presidium of the USSR Supreme Soviet rejected the transfer in July 1988. The Sumgait pogrom of 27–29 February 1988 was followed by violence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganja-azerbaijan">Kirovabad</a> that November and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> in January 1990. After the Armenian earthquake of 7 December 1988 the members of the Karabakh Committee were arrested and taken to Moscow; they were freed on 31 May 1989. In January 1989 Moscow placed the region under a special administration answerable to the central government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The committee's following became the Pan-Armenian National Movement, which won a majority in the parliamentary election of 20 May 1990. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levon-ter-petrosyan">Levon Ter-Petrosyan</a> was elected chairman of the Supreme Council on 4 August 1990, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> declared independence on 23 August. In 1991 Soviet and Azerbaijani forces deported Armenian villagers in Operation Ring, the Nagorno-Karabakh Republic was proclaimed on 2 September, and in November Azerbaijan voted to abolish the region's autonomy. By 1992 the conflict had become the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war">First Nagorno-Karabakh War</a>.</p><p>About 350,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, and some 167,000 Azerbaijanis left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> from the autumn of 1988. Armenians regard the movement as a struggle for national liberation, and it was the largest public mobilisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> since the 1920s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karabakh movement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under glasnost the <b>Karabakh movement</b> brought nearly a million demonstrators to Yerevan in 1988; the Spitak earthquake of 1988 killed more than 25,000 people, and the Baku pogrom of January 1990 drove some 200,000 Armenians to Armenia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Demonstrations in Yerevan's Theater Square in support grew into the <b>Karabakh movement</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1988 the <b>Karabakh movement</b> mobilised Armenians to demand Nagorno-Karabakh, while the Spitak earthquake killed about 25,000 people.</blockquote><a href="/state/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1988 the <b>Karabakh movement</b> led to war with Armenians, and Soviet troops killed over a hundred civilians in Baku in January 1990.</blockquote><a href="/state/azerbaijan-ssr">Azerbaijan SSR</a></li></ul></section>
